### Why Edge-Triggered Mode Requires Exhaustive Draining
In level-triggered mode, epoll reminds the application repeatedly if data remains unread in the socket buffer. In edge-triggered (`EPOLLET`) mode, notifications are only sent when a state transition occurs on the file descriptor (e.g., new data arrives). If an application fails to read *all* available data from the kernel socket buffer until `EAGAIN` or `EWOULDBLOCK` is returned, no further notifications will be triggered for remaining data until a *new* packet arrives, causing potential hangs.

### Complete C Implementation
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <errno.h>

typedef struct {
    char *data;
    size_t size;
    size_t capacity;
} DynamicBuffer;

int init_buffer(DynamicBuffer *buf, size_t initial_capacity) {
    buf->data = malloc(initial_capacity);
    if (!buf->data) return -1;
    buf->size = 0;
    buf->capacity = initial_capacity;
    return 0;
}

void free_buffer(DynamicBuffer *buf) {
    free(buf->data);
    buf->data = NULL;
    buf->size = 0;
    buf->capacity = 0;
}

int drain_socket_to_buffer(int fd, DynamicBuffer *buf) {
    char temp_stack[4096];
    while (1) {
        ssize_t n = read(fd, temp_stack, sizeof(temp_stack));
        if (n < 0) {
            if (errno == EAGAIN || errno == EWOULDBLOCK) {
                return 0;
            }
            if (errno == EINTR) {
                continue;
            }
            return -1;
        } else if (n == 0) {
            return 1;
        }

        if (buf->size + n > buf->capacity) {
            size_t new_capacity = buf->capacity * 2;
            while (buf->size + n > new_capacity) {
                new_capacity *= 2;
            }
            char *new_data = realloc(buf->data, new_capacity);
            if (!new_data) {
                return -1;
            }
            buf->data = new_data;
            buf->capacity = new_capacity;
        }

        for (ssize_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            buf->data[buf->size + i] = temp_stack[i];
        }
        buf->size += n;
    }
}

int main(void) {
    printf("Buffer drainage module compiled successfully.\n");
    return 0;
}
```

### Complexity Analysis
- **Time Complexity**: $O(N)$ relative to the number of bytes available in the kernel socket receive queue. Buffer resizing via geometric growth (`realloc`) executes in amortized $O(1)$ time per chunk.
- **Space Complexity**: $O(M)$ where $M$ is the total number of bytes read and accumulated into the dynamic buffer.

