## 1. Root-Cause Analysis: The ABA Problem and Use-After-Free

The intermittent segmentation faults occur due to the classic ABA problem compounded by unsafe memory reclamation in lock-free node pop operations. When Thread 1 reads head pointer $A$, and gets preempted, Thread 2 pops node $A$, pops node $B$, and reuses the memory address of node $A$ for a newly allocated node $C$. When Thread 1 resumes, its CAS (Compare-And-Swap) operation sees address $A$ and succeeds, assuming the queue state hasn't changed. However, the internal pointers of node $A$ are now corrupt or dangling, leading to null-pointer dereferences or segmentation faults.

## 2. Mathematical Formalization of Hazard Pointers

Let $H_t$ be the set of hazard pointers published by thread $t$, protecting memory addresses from immediate reclamation. A node $n$ allocated at address $p$ can only be freed when:

$$\forall t \in T, \quad p \notin H_t$$

This guarantees safety by deferring memory freeing until no active thread holds a read reference to $p$.

