## 1. Root-Cause Analysis: Unbounded Priority Inversion

Priority inversion occurs when a high-priority thread ($T_H$) is indirectly preempted by a medium-priority thread ($T_M$) because a low-priority thread ($T_L$) holds a critical resource needed by $T_H$. 

1. $T_L$ acquires mutex $M$.
2. $T_H$ preempts $T_L$, attempts to acquire $M$, and blocks. $T_L$ inherits no priority and remains blocked by CPU scheduling.
3. $T_M$ arrives, preempts $T_L$ due to intermediate priority ($Priority(T_L) < Priority(T_M) < Priority(T_H)$).
4. $T_H$ is now blocked indefinitely by $T_M$, despite having a strictly higher priority, violating real-time deterministic guarantees.

## 2. Mathematical Response Time Formulation with Priority Inheritance

Let $R_i$ be the worst-case response time of task $i$. Under Priority Inheritance Protocol (PIP), when a high-priority task blocks on a resource held by a lower-priority task, the lower-priority task inherits the priority of the highest-priority blocked task.

The blocking term $B_i$, representing the maximum time task $i$ can be blocked by lower-priority tasks sharing resources, is bounded by:

$$B_i = \sum_{j 	oresources} C_j$$

Where $C_j$ is the critical section execution time of resource-holding task $j$. Priority inheritance guarantees that intermediate tasks cannot preempt $T_L$ while it holds the critical resource, bounding the inversion duration to the length of the critical section.

