In multi-core SMP architectures, frequent atomic updates (`std::atomic::fetch_add` or CAS loops) on shared control-block counters invalidate L1/L2 CPU caches across all cores via the MESI/MOESI cache coherence protocols. When multiple threads repeatedly write to distinct variables residing within the same 64-byte cache line, false sharing occurs, inducing massive bus contention and stalling pipeline execution.

## 2. Mathematical Modeling of RCU Grace Periods

Let $GP$ be a global grace period counter. A reader entering a critical section registers its current CPU/thread context. An updater modifying shared data structures publishes a new pointer version $V_{new}$ and increments $GP$. A grace period is complete when all active readers pre-dating $V_{new}$ have exited their critical sections:

$$\forall r \in \text{ActiveReaders}, \quad t_{entry}(r) > t_{update}(V_{new})$$

