The ABA problem manifests in lock-free pointer-swapping loops (e.g., CAS-based pop operations) when a thread reads value $A$ from a shared node pointer, gets preempted, while other threads perform operations that pop node $A$, free its memory, allocate a new node at the exact same virtual memory address containing value $B$, and eventually push node $A$ back onto the structure containing value $A$. When the original thread resumes, its Compare-And-Swap (CAS) operation succeeds because the pointer matches $A$, completely missing the intermediate structural mutations.

Let history $H$ be a sequence of invocation and response events. Linearizability requires that every concurrent execution maps to a valid sequential history equivalent to a global partial order respecting real-time precedence. The ABA violation breaks linearizability by permitting invalid node traversals past unlinked or freed memory bounds, triggering undefined behavior or memory corruption.

