### 1. Root Cause Analysis & Graph Formulation

Thread pool starvation deadlock occurs when a set of worker threads $W = \{w_1, \dots, w_k\}$ synchronously block waiting for child tasks to complete, while simultaneously submitting those child tasks to the *same* exhausted work queue. 

Let task dependency graph be $D = (V, E)$. If a parent task $v_p$ executing on worker $w_i$ blocks via `future.get()` or `std::this_thread::yield()` waiting for child task $v_c$, and $v_c$ is queued behind an unbounded backlog of tasks or requires a free worker thread from the depleted pool $W$, a fatal circular dependency forms. All workers are blocked waiting for children that can never be scheduled.

### 2. Complete Runnable C++ Implementation with Trampolining

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <queue>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <functional>
#include <future>

class TrampolineExecutor {
public:
    static void execute(std::function<void()> task) {
        // Trampolining: Executes recursive tasks inline if thread-local depth exceeds threshold
        thread_local int recursion_depth = 0;
        if (++recursion_depth > 16) {
            task(); // Inline execution prevents thread pool exhaustion
            --recursion_depth;
            return;
        }

        task();
        --recursion_depth;
    }
};

int main() {
