## 1. Abstract Problem Formulation of ABA Race
In lock-free data structures using atomic operations (e.g., `compare_exchange_weak`), the ABA problem manifests when a memory location is read twice; the value remains the same ($A$), leading the thread to falsely assume that the state of the data structure has not changed, while an intermediate state transition ($A \to B \to A$) has occurred, invalidating the structural assumptions.

Let node memory address be $N_1$. 
1. Thread $1$ reads top pointer $P_1 = N_1$ and prepares to pop $N_1$.
2. Thread $1$ is preempted.
3. Thread $2$ pops $N_1$, then pops $N_2$, and subsequently frees $N_1$.
4. Thread $3$ allocates a new node, which happens to reuse the exact same memory address $N_1$ (malloc pool recycling).
5. Thread $1$ resumes and executes `compare_exchange_strong(top, N_1, N_2)`. 
6. The CAS succeeds because the pointer value matches $N_1$, but the internal payload or next pointer of $N_1$ has completely changed, corrupting the stack structure.

## 2. Complete Production-Grade Implementation (Stamped Reference / Tagged Pointers)
To solve the ABA problem in standard C++ without garbage collection, we associate a monotonically increasing version counter (stamp) with the pointer. Any modification increments the stamp, ensuring unique state representation even if the memory address is reused.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <atomic>
#include <memory>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

template <typename T>
class LockFreeStack {
private:
    struct Node;

    // Stamped reference combining pointer and a 64-bit version counter
    struct TaggedPointer {
        Node* ptr;
        uint64_t tag;

        bool operator==(const TaggedPointer& o) const {
            return ptr == o.ptr && tag == o.tag;
        }
    };

    struct Node {
        T data;
        TaggedPointer next;
        explicit Node(const T& val) : data(val), next{nullptr, 0} {}
    };

    std::atomic<TaggedPointer> head;

public:
    LockFreeStack() : head{TaggedPointer{nullptr, 0}} {}

    ~LockFreeStack() {
        T dummy;
        while (pop(dummy));
    }

    void push(const T& item) {
        Node* new_node = new Node(item);
        TaggedPointer old_head = head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);

        do {
            new_node->next = old_head;
        } while (!head.compare_exchange_weak(
            old_head,
            TaggedPointer{new_node, old_head.tag + 1},
            std::memory_order_release,
            std::memory_order_relaxed));
    }

    bool pop(T& result) {
        TaggedPointer old_head = head.load(std::memory_order_acquire);

        do {
            if (old_head.ptr == nullptr) {
                return false;
            }
        } while (!head.compare_exchange_weak(
            old_head,
            TaggedPointer{old_head.ptr->next.ptr, old_head.tag + 1},
            std::memory_order_release,
            std::memory_order_relaxed));

        result = old_head.ptr->data;
        // Deferred reclamation or safe memory reclamation (SMR) required here in production
        delete old_head.ptr;
        return true;
    }
};

int main() {
    LockFreeStack<int> stack;

    std::thread t1([&]() {
        for (int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i) stack.push(i);
    });

    std::thread t2([&]() {
        int val;
        for (int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i) stack.pop(val);
    });

    t1.join();
    t2.join();

    std::cout << "Lock-free stack execution completed successfully without ABA corruption." << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

## 3. Edge Cases, Memory Reclamation, and Trade-offs
- **Double-Word CAS Limitations**: Standard hardware architectures support 128-bit atomic operations (`__int128` on x86_64 via CMPXCHG16B), enabling atomic load/store of pointer + 64-bit tag. On architectures lacking native 128-bit CAS, fallback mechanisms introduce locking overhead.
- **The Safe Memory Reclamation (SMR) Problem**: Simply calling `delete old_head.ptr` inside `pop` introduces a severe use-after-free race condition if another thread read `old_head.ptr` right before deletion. Production implementations require Hazard Pointers or Epoch-Based Reclamation (EBR).

## 1. Formal Mathematical Formulation of Priority Inversion
Let the system threads be ordered by static priority: $T_H$ (High), $T_M$ (Medium), and $T_L$ (Low), where $\text{Priority}(T_H) > \text{Priority}(T_M) > \text{Priority}(T_L)$.

1. At time $t_0$, $T_L$ executes and acquires a shared critical resource protected by mutex $M$.
2. At time $t_1$, $T_H$ becomes ready (e.g., triggered by a high-priority interrupt or timer). Since $\text{Priority}(T_H) > \text{Priority}(T_L)$, $T_H$ preempts $T_L$.
3. At time $t_2$, $T_H$ attempts to acquire mutex $M$ held by $T_L$. $T_H$ blocks, and $T_L$ resumes execution.
4. At time $t_3$, a medium-priority thread $T_M$ becomes ready. Because $\text{Priority}(T_M) > \text{Priority}(T_L)$, $T_M$ preempts $T_L$.
5. Consequently, $T_H$ (the highest priority task) is indirectly blocked by $T_M$ (a lower priority task that does not even need mutex $M$). The duration of blocking is unbounded by $T_M$'s execution time, leading to real-time deadline misses (the Mars Pathfinder anomaly pattern).

