To derive the expected time complexity of Randomized QuickSelect for finding the $k$-th smallest element, let $X_i$ be an indicator random variable that equals 1 if the chosen pivot partitions the array such that the size of the chosen subproblem is at least a fraction $	extstyle rac{3}{4}$ of the previous size, and 0 otherwise. The probability that a pivot falls in the central 50% of the sorted array is $1/2$. Thus, the expected number of steps at each level is bounded by a geometric series. Let $T(n)$ be the expected time. We can write $T(n) ≤ T(3n/4) + O(n)$. By applying the substitution method or the master theorem variant for expected values, we find that the expected time complexity is $E[T(n)] = O(n)$. However, the worst-case time complexity is $O(n^2)$ when consistently bad pivots are chosen. 

To guarantee a deterministic $O(n)$ worst-case bound, we use the Median-of-Medians algorithm. Below is a complete, runnable implementation of the Median-of-Medians selection algorithm in Python.

