Architectural Trade-offs: While Median-of-Medians guarantees a strict $O(n)$ worst-case bound, its practical runtime constant factor is significantly higher than Randomized QuickSelect due to recursive sorting of 5-element sublists and multiple list comprehensions allocating new memory allocations. In production systems (e.g., C++ `std::nth_element`), introspective selection algorithms (IntroSelect) are preferred, starting with QuickSelect for expected $O(n)$ performance and falling back to Median-of-Medians only when recursion depth exceeds a strict threshold.

Step 1: Root-Cause Analysis via MESI Protocol and False Sharing. When multiple threads modify distinct variables that reside within the same 64-byte cache line, hardware cache coherence protocols (MESI/MOESI) enforce serial access. If Core A modifies variable X and Core B modifies variable Y, and both X and Y occupy bytes 16 and 32 of Cache Line L, the line transitions between Modified (M) and Invalid (I) states across CPU sockets or core L1/L2 caches. This phenomenon, known as false sharing, causes high-latency cache line bouncing through the L3 cache or ring bus. Step 2: Mathematical Formulation of Latency Penalty. Let $T_{hit}$ be the L1 cache hit latency (approx 1 ns), $T_{L2}$ be the L2 hit latency (approx 4 ns), $T_{L3}$ be the L3 hit latency (approx 15 ns), and $T_{remote}$ be the inter-socket NUMA access latency (approx 60 ns). Under heavy false sharing, every write operation forces an invalidation broadcast. The expected write latency becomes $E[T_{write}] = p_{L2}T_{L2} + p_{L3}T_{L3} + p_{remote}T_{remote}$, escalating operational latency by over 4000% compared to localized L1 writes. Step 3: Mitigation via Alignment and SoA. We apply `alignas(64)` to isolate variables to distinct cache lines and transition from Array-of-Structures (AoS) to Structure-of-Arrays (SoA) to guarantee spatial locality and eliminate inter-thread contention. Step 4: Complete Runnable Implementation. ```cpp
#include <thread>
