Step 1: Architectural Analysis of Hardware Prefetchers. Modern CPU architectures implement aggressive hardware prefetchers (such as the Data Stream Detector and Spatial Prefetcher) that monitor L1/L2 cache misses to anticipate sequential memory access patterns. When streaming contiguous memory (e.g., contiguous arrays), prefetchers load upcoming cache lines into L2/L1d caches, hiding memory latency. Step 2: Failure Mode in Pointer-Chasing. In sparse pointer-chasing structures (e.g., `std::list` or linked nodes), memory addresses are non-contiguous. The hardware prefetcher fails to detect a deterministic stride, resulting in high L1/L2 cache miss rates. Furthermore, prefetchers may mispredict patterns, bringing useless cache lines into the hierarchy, evicting hot working sets, and saturating memory bus bandwidth. Step 3: Mathematical Model of Bandwidth Saturation. Effective bandwidth $B_{eff} =  rac{Bytes_{useful}}{Time} 	imes ( rac{Bytes_{useful}}{Bytes_{useful} + Bytes_{wasted}})$. In pointer chasing, $Bytes_{wasted}$ approaches infinity relative to useful payload per fetch, dropping efficiency below 5%. Step 4: Complete Runnable Demonstration. ```cpp
