## 3. Trade-offs and Edge Cases

1. **Random Access Penalty**: While SoA excels in sequential streaming updates, random access (e.g., entity-to-entity collision resolution based on spatial hashing) requires pointer chasing across multiple separate vectors, destroying cache locality.
2. **Code Complexity**: SoA requires complex memory management across multiple vectors and inhibits object-oriented encapsulation. Solutions like `AoS of SoA` (Array of Structures of Arrays / Chunked SoA) are often deployed in production game engines to balance streaming throughput with random access clustering.

## 1. Mathematical Analysis of Conflict Misses and Cache Aliasing

CPU caches are organized into sets. A cache with capacity $C$, line size $L$, and associativity $A$ has $S = C / (A 	imes L)$ sets. A memory address $M$ is mapped to a cache set index $I$ via:

$$I = \left( \lfloor M / L \rfloor \right) \pmod S$$

### Conflict Misses and Power-of-Two Strides
When operating on matrices where dimensions are powers of two (e.g., $1024 \times 1024$ floats), row strides often map to exact multiples of the cache set count. If multiple active data structures map to the exact same cache set index while exceeding associativity $A$, the cache replacement policy (e.g., Least Recently Used) evicts valid lines prematurely.

This phenomenon—**cache line aliasing**—turns an $O(N)$ memory sweep into a thrashing cycle where lines are repeatedly fetched from L3 or RAM, resulting in near-zero effective cache hit rates despite small working sets.

---

## 2. Runnable Demonstration of Cache Line Aliasing

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <chrono>

// A power-of-two stride often triggers severe cache aliasing in direct-mapped or low-associativity caches.
constexpr size_t MATRIX_SIZE = 4096;

int main() {
    // Allocate a large matrix
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> matrix(MATRIX_SIZE, std::vector<double>(MATRIX_SIZE, 1.0));

    // Column-major traversal with power-of-two stride causes catastrophic cache conflict misses
    auto start = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();

    double sum = 0.0;
    for (size_t col = 0; col < MATRIX_SIZE; ++col) {
        for (size_t row = 0; row < MATRIX_SIZE; ++row) {
            sum += matrix[row][col];
        }
    }

    auto end = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
    auto duration = std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::milliseconds>(end - start).count();

    std::cout << "Aliased Column-Major Sum Result: " << sum << "\n";
    std::cout << "Execution Time (Aliased Stride): " << duration << " ms\n";

    // Mitigation: Matrix padding or loop tiling
    return 0;
}
```

