### 4. Trade-Offs, Edge Cases, and Proofs

* **Spatial Locality vs. Inter-thread Contention Trade-off**: AoS maximizes spatial locality when a *single* thread reads/writes all fields of a single record sequentially (fetching order ID, price, quantity in one cache line fetch). However, when *multiple* threads mutate distinct fields concurrently, AoS introduces destructive interference (false sharing). SoA eliminates inter-thread contention entirely at the expense of scattering memory accesses across different memory regions when reading *all* attributes of a single record.
* **Hardware Prefetcher Considerations**: Modern CPU hardware stream prefetchers detect sequential memory access patterns (common in SoA loops). If array strides match cache line boundaries (64 bytes), prefetchers load subsequent arrays into L2/L1d caches proactively, mitigating the higher latency cost of scattered memory access.
* **Alignment Proof**: Using `alignas(64)` guarantees that no two distinct array allocations or struct members share the same 64-byte boundary, satisfying the strict requirements of the x86-64 System V Application Binary Interface and preventing cross-core cache invalidation storms.

### 1. Mathematical Formulation of TLB Walk Penalties

Modern x86-64 processors support 4-level paging (PML4 -> PDPT -> PD -> PT -> Physical Frame) and 5-level paging (PML5). Each level requires a memory access if the Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB) misses.

Let:
- $L_{1\text{dtlb}}$ = L1 Data TLB hit latency (~1 cycle)
- $L_{2\text{dtlb}}$ = L2 Data TLB hit latency (~7-10 cycles)
- $T_{\text{walk}}$ = Cost of a full page table walk on L1/L2 TLB miss
- $N_{\text{levels}}$ = 4 (for 4KB pages) or 3 (for 2MB Huge Pages)
- $C_{\text{mem}}$ = L3/RAM access latency (~40-250 cycles per level traversal)

The average memory access latency with TLB misses is:

$$\text{Latency} = P_{\text{hit}} \times L_{\text{dtlb}} + (1 - P_{\text{hit}}) \times \left( L_{\text{dtlb}} + N_{\text{levels}} \times C_{\text{mem}} \right)$$

When working set size exceeds the 4KB TLB capacity (typically 64 entries for L1 DT LB, 1536 entries for L2 DT LB = ~6MB total coverage), $P_{\text{hit}} \to 0$. A 4-level page walk penalty can reach 300-400 CPU cycles per pointer dereference, stalling the out-of-order execution engine completely.

### 2. Complete Runnable C++20 Implementation (Huge Pages vs 4KB Pages)

