## 2. Root Cause Analysis (RCA)

### 2.1 Page Pinning and MMU Invalidation Race
When a driver pins pages using `get_user_pages()` (GUP), it increments the page reference count (`page->_refcount`). However, pinning a page via GUP does *not* prevent user-space from unmapping the virtual address (`munmap`) or altering page permissions via `mprotect`.

1. Driver pins virtual page $V$ at physical frame $P$.
2. User thread executes `munmap(V, size)`, clearing the PTE in the page table and executing an explicit or implicit TLB shootdown across active cores.
3. The physical page $P$ remains allocated because its reference count $> 0$, but the virtual-to-physical mapping no longer exists in the page tables.
4. When the kernel driver or device attempts to perform a copy or reference the virtual address directly without re-validating the mapping, the CPU triggers a page fault because the hardware page table walk fails.

### 2.2 Mathematical Formulation of the Invalidation Window
Let $T_{	ext{GUP}}$ be the timestamp of the pin operation, $T_{	ext{unmap}}$ be the timestamp of the user-space unmap request, and $T_{	ext{DMA}}$ be the completion timestamp. The vulnerability manifests when:
$$T_{	ext{GUP}} < T_{	ext{unmap}} < T_{	ext{DMA}}$$ 
During this window, the page table entry is marked *not present* ($P=0$), yet the driver treats the virtual address as valid.

## 3. Production-Grade Mitigation Implementation

To resolve this, drivers must integrate with the MMU notifier subsystem (`mmu_notifier`) to intercept invalidations, unmaps, and range changes before they invalidate active mappings.

```c
#drivers/char/secure_dma_driver.c
#include <linux/module.h>
#include <linux/kernel.h>
#include <linux/mmu_notifier.h>
#include <linux/sched/mm.h>
#include <linux/pagemap.h>

struct secure_dma_dev {
    struct mmu_notifier mn;
    struct mm_struct *mm;
    unsigned long start;
    unsigned long end;
    struct page **pinned_pages;
    int nr_pages;
};

static void secure_dma_invalidate_range_start(struct mmu_notifier *mn,
                                            const struct mmu_notifier_range *range)
{
    struct secure_dma_dev *dev = container_of(mn, struct secure_dma_dev, mn);

    if (range->start < dev->end && range->end > dev->start) {
        // Synchronize and abort or flush active DMA before user space unmaps memory
        pr_warn("SecureDMA: MMU invalidation detected in active range [%lx, %lx]\n",
                range->start, range->end);
        // Hardware DMA cancellation logic goes here
        dma_sync_wait_for_completion();
    }
}

static const struct mmu_notifier_ops secure_dma_mmu_ops = {
    .invalidate_range_start = secure_dma_invalidate_range_start,
};

int register_secure_dma_region(struct secure_dma_dev *dev, unsigned long addr, size_t size)
{
    int ret;

    dev->mm = current->mm;
    dev->start = addr;
    dev->end = addr + size;
    dev->nr_pages = size >> PAGE_SHIFT;

    dev->pinned_pages = kmalloc_array(dev->nr_pages, sizeof(struct page *), GFP_KERNEL);
    if (!dev->pinned_pages)
        return -ENOMEM;

    mmap_read_lock(dev->mm);
    ret = pin_user_pages(addr, dev->nr_pages, FOLL_WRITE | FOLL_LONGTERM, dev->pinned_pages);
    mmap_read_unlock(dev->mm);

    if (ret < 0) {
        kfree(dev->pinned_pages);
        return ret;
    }

    dev->mn.ops = &secure_dma_mmu_ops;
    return mmu_notifier_register(&dev->mn, dev->mm);
}
```

