## 2. Root Cause Analysis (RCA)

### 2.1 SLUB Heap Layout and Overflow Mechanics
In the Linux SLUB allocator, objects of similar sizes are packed contiguously into memory pages. Unlike user-space allocators (like modern hardened jemalloc or hardened_malloc), standard kernel SLUB heaps do not inherently interleave randomized guard pages between every single small allocation due to performance overhead.

When a buffer overflow occurs:
1. Object $A$ is allocated with size $N$. Buffer $A$ is adjacent to Object $B$ in the same slab page.
2. The `ioctl` handler executes `copy_from_user(A->buffer, user_buf, user_size)` where `user_size > N`.
3. Bytes exceeding $N$ overwrite the header or payload of Object $B$.
4. If Object $B$ contains function pointers (e.g., file operations structures, work struct callbacks), an attacker can hijack control flow, or the kernel panics instantly upon detecting corrupted redzone bytes (`0xcc` or KASAN poisoning tags).

### 2.2 Mathematical Bound of Validation
Let $U_{	ext{size}}$ be the size requested by user-space, and $K_{	ext{size}}$ be the allocated size of the destination buffer $D$. The safety condition required to prevent heap corruption is strictly:
$$U_{	ext{size}} \le \text{ksize}(D)$$
where `ksize()` returns the actual rounded-up usable size of the SLUB allocation object.

## 3. Production-Grade Mitigation Implementation

To permanently eliminate bounds corruption, we implement rigorous validation combining `array_index_nospec()` protections, explicit bounds checking against `ksize()`, and modern safe copy primitives.

```c
#drivers/char/secure_chardev.c
#include <linux/module.h>
#include <linux/kernel.h>
#include <linux/fs.h>
#include <linux/uaccess.h>
#include <linux/slab.h>
#include <linux/nospec.h>

#define MAX_COMMAND_PAYLOAD_SIZE 1024

struct device_payload {
    size_t length;
    u8 data[0];
};

long secure_ioctl_handler(struct file *file, unsigned int cmd, unsigned long arg)
{
    void __user *argp = (void __user *)arg;
    struct device_payload user_hdr;
    struct device_payload *kernel_buf;
    size_t actual_alloc_size;
    long ret = 0;

    if (copy_from_user(&user_hdr, argp, sizeof(user_hdr)))
        return -EFAULT;

    // 1. Strict boundary validation
    if (user_hdr.length > MAX_COMMAND_PAYLOAD_SIZE) 
        return -EINVAL;

    // Sanitize against speculative execution side-channels
    user_hdr.length = array_index_nospec(user_hdr.length, MAX_COMMAND_PAYLOAD_SIZE + 1);

    // 2. Allocate memory with explicit tracking
    kernel_buf = kmalloc(struct_size(kernel_buf, data, user_hdr.length), GFP_KERNEL);
    if (!kernel_buf)
        return -ENOMEM;

    kernel_buf->length = user_hdr.length;

    // 3. Query actual SLUB allocation size to verify boundaries
    actual_alloc_size = ksize(kernel_buf);
    if (unlikely(sizeof(struct device_payload) + user_hdr.length > actual_alloc_size)) {
        ret = -EOVERFLOW;
        goto out_free;
    }

    // 4. Safe copy with verified bounds
    if (copy_from_user(kernel_buf->data, argp + sizeof(user_hdr), user_hdr.length)) {
        ret = -EFAULT;
        goto out_free;
    }

    // Process validated payload...

out_free:
    kzfree(kernel_buf);
    return ret;
}
```

