### 1. Symptom & Failure Manifestation
The system crashes with a kernel panic showing `Kernel panic - not syncing: Fatal exception in interrupt` or an unhandled Page Fault in high memory addresses corresponding to the end of the thread's stack. KASAN (Kernel Address Sanitizer) reports a stack-out-of-bounds access where the thread stack pointer ($rsp$) breaches the guard page region (`THREAD_SIZE` boundary, typically 16KB on x86_64).

### 2. Vulnerability Mechanism
The filesystem implements recursive directory tree walking:
```c
int traverse_dir(struct dentry *dentry, int depth) {
    char local_buffer[PATH_MAX];
    // ... recursive descent ...
    return traverse_dir(child, depth + 1);
}
```
For deeply nested directory structures ($depth > 500$), cumulative stack frame allocations ($500 \times 4KB = 2MB$) exceed the fixed physical kernel stack allocation per task ($16KB$). This overwrites adjacent memory domains, corrupting thread descriptors (`struct task_struct`) stored immediately adjacent in memory.

### 3. Mathematical Stack Depth Formulation
Let $F$ be the stack frame size of the recursive function, and $S_{max}$ be the maximum allowed kernel stack size ($16384$ bytes). Let overhead per frame including register saves be $O$. The maximum safe recursion depth $D_{safe}$ is bounded by:
$$\sum_{i=1}^{D_{safe}} (F_i + O) \le S_{max} - S_{reserved}$$
Where $S_{reserved}$ accounts for interrupt handler stack usage (approx. $4KB$). If average $F = 256$ bytes and $O = 64$ bytes, $D_{safe} \le \frac{12288}{320} \approx 38$ levels of recursion. Any depth exceeding 38 deterministically corrupts memory.

### 4. Corrected Implementation (Iterative Traversal with Explicit Heap State)
Below is the complete, runnable kernel-safe iterative traversal implementation using an explicit heap-allocated stack queue to eliminate recursion entirely.

```c
#include <linux/module.h>
#include <linux/kernel.h>
#include <linux/init.h>
#include <linux/slab.h>
#include <linux/fs.h>
#include <linux/dcache.h>
#include <linux/list.h>

MODULE_LICENSE(
"GPL");
MODULE_AUTHOR("Principal Architect");
MODULE_DESCRIPTION("Iterative Directory Traversal preventing Stack Overflow");

struct traversal_node {
    struct dentry *dentry;
    struct list_head list;
};

int safe_iterative_traverse(struct dentry *root_dentry) {
    struct list_head stack;
    struct traversal_node *current_node, *next_node;
    int err = 0;

    INIT_LIST_HEAD(&stack);

    current_node = kmalloc(sizeof(*current_node), GFP_KERNEL);
    if (!current_node)
        return -ENOMEM;

    current_node->dentry = root_dentry;
    list_add(&current_node->list, &stack);

    while (!list_empty(&stack)) {
        current_node = list_first_entry(&stack, struct traversal_node, list);
        list_del(&current_node->list);

        // Process current dentry safely on heap
        if (current_node->dentry) {
            pr_info("Processing dentry name: %pd\n", current_node->dentry);

            // Example: Push child entries to heap stack instead of recursive call
            spin_lock(&current_node->dentry->d_lock);
            if (!list_empty(&current_node->dentry->d_subdirs)) {
                struct dentry *child;
                list_for_each_entry(child, &current_node->dentry->d_subdirs, d_child) {
                    struct traversal_node *child_node = kmalloc(sizeof(*child_node), GFP_KERNEL);
                    if (child_node) {
                        child_node->dentry = child;
                        list_add(&child_node->list, &stack);
                    } else {
                        err = -ENOMEM;
                        spin_unlock(&current_node->dentry->d_lock);
                        goto cleanup;
                    }
                }
            }
            spin_unlock(&current_node->dentry->d_lock);
        }

        kfree(current_node);
    }

cleanup:
    // Drain remaining stack on error
    list_for_each_entry_safe(current_node, next_node, &stack, list) {
        list_del(&current_node->list);
        kfree(current_node);
    }

    return err;
}

static int __init stack_rca_init(void) {
    pr_info("Stack overflow RCA module loaded\n");
    return 0;
}

static void __exit stack_rca_exit(void) {
    pr_info("Stack overflow RCA module unloaded\n");
}

module_init(stack_rca_init);
module_exit(stack_rca_exit);
```

### 5. Trade-offs, Edge Cases & Failure Modes
- **Heap Allocation Overhead:** Replacing stack frames with heap allocations (`kmalloc`) shifts pressure from CPU cache and stack space to the SLUB allocator, increasing latency.
- **Memory Exhaustion:** Under severe low-memory conditions (`-ENOMEM`), pushing nodes to the heap stack can fail, requiring robust error unwinding to prevent memory leaks.
- **Virtually Indexed Stacks:** Modern kernels support `CONFIG_VMAP_STACK`, which places kernel stacks in virtually contiguous memory separated by unmapped guard pages, causing an immediate page fault and clean kernel oops instead of silent memory corruption when limits are breached.

