## Root Cause Analysis: SLUB Heap Corruption via Off-by-One Overflow

### 1. Symptom & Failure Manifestation
The kernel experiences erratic behavior, silent data corruption, and sporadic panics in unrelated kernel subsystems (`RCU stall`, `Corrupted double-linked list`, or `slab corruption: kfree validation failed`). Debugging with SLUB debugging options enabled (`slub_debug=FPUZ`) reveals explicit corruption stamps: `Redzone overwritten` or `Freelist pointer corrupted` in `mm/slub.c`.

### 2. Vulnerability Mechanism
The IPC message handler copies user-space payloads into kernel heap objects using a flawed length calculation:
```c
struct ipc_message {
    size_t length;
    char payload[64];
};

int receive_ipc_message(const char __user *user_buf, size_t user_len) {
    struct ipc_message *msg = kmalloc(sizeof(*msg), GFP_KERNEL);
    if (!msg)
        return -ENOMEM;

    // BUG: Off-by-one error: user_len <= sizeof(msg->payload) instead of <
    if (user_len > sizeof(msg->payload)) {
        kfree(msg);
        return -EINVAL;
    }

    // If user_len == 65 due to incorrect boundary check, copy_from_user overflows payload[64]
    if (copy_from_user(msg->payload, user_buf, user_len))
        return -EFAULT;

    msg->length = user_len;
    return 0;
}
```
When `user_len = 65`, `copy_from_user` writes 1 byte past the end of `msg->payload`. In the SLUB allocator, small objects are packed tightly. Writing 1 byte past the object bounds overwrites the adjacent object's header or the SLUB freelist pointer (`freelist` pointer embedded in the first word of free objects).

### 3. SLUB Metadata Destruction Analysis
When an object is freed, SLUB stores the pointer to the next free object inside the first bytes of the object payload area. If an off-by-one overflow corrupts this pointer from `0xffff888102345000` to `0xffff888102345058`, the next allocation (`kmalloc`) retrieves the corrupted pointer, leading the allocator to treat an arbitrary memory address as a freelist pointer and dereferencing it during subsequent allocations.

### 4. Corrected Implementation
